Initial report (The Daily News, November 22, 1929): &#34;Fifteen families homelss, all fishing property with provisions and coal swept away, four lives lost...&#34; Eleven homes were destroyed. On November 24, The Daily News reports: &#34;This is the worst place on the whole coast and the tidal wave was between 80 to 100 feet high, which carried everything in its way. Houses were shifted in every direction and some completely destroyed.&#34; November 27 report: &#34;At Point au Gaul the tidal wave was described as having a rotary motion rolling on the shore in a mass foam while at Taylor&#39;s Bay eye witnesses describe it as rising so high that it blotted out the stars.&#34;
<p>Tuttle et al. (Reference #1830) conducted field measurements and determined that the tsunami was up to 7 m high, and Ruffman (Reference #1832) states that the runup at the head of Taylor&#39;s Bay was about 13 m. Five died in the event, and one died from injuries shortly thereafter (Reference #1719).
</p><p>The height and forward momentum of the arriving tsunami caused the runup to rise to as much as 13 m above sealevel at the ends of the long narrow harbours such as Port au Bras, St. Lawrence, Little Lawn Harbour, Lawn, Lord&#39;s Cove, Taylor&#39;s Bay, and Lamaline. (reference #1832)</p>

Maximum tsunami height of 7.7 m was measured at Farusi, Wardo, W.Biak which is located at the back side of the tsunami source. (reference #430)
<P>Surprisingly, the maximum tsunami height of 7.7 m was measured at Farusi, Wardo, in western Biak, which faces away
from the tsunami source. Tsunami heights of 1.6 to 7.7 m were measured in this region.  (reference #163)

